-- title:
--   Auslander–Buchsbaum: regular local rings are factorial
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring that is an integral domain and a local ring, and assume $R$ is regular in Mathlib's sense (`IsRegularRing`): $R$ is Noetherian and the localisation of $R$ at every prime ideal is a regular local ring; in particular, since $R$ is local, $R$ itself is a regular local ring, its maximal ideal being generated by a system of parameters of length equal to its Krull dimension. The conclusion is that $R$ is a unique factorisation monoid, i.e. $R$ satisfies Mathlib's `UniqueFactorizationMonoid`: the divisibility relation on $R$ is well-founded and every irreducible element of $R$ is prime, equivalently every nonzero non-unit of $R$ factors as a product of irreducibles, uniquely up to order and units. Thus $R$ is a unique factorisation domain. Note that the regularity hypothesis is imposed on all localisations of $R$ rather than only on $R$ itself; the two forms are equivalent by Serre's theorem, but the stated form is the one available for local rings of schemes with regular stalks.
--
--   This is the theorem of Auslander and Buchsbaum that regular local rings are unique factorisation domains. Within the project it supplies factoriality of local rings of regular schemes, and is used in the construction of invertible modules and sections on affine opens, in the discrete valuation ring step for abelian schemes, and in producing suitable elements of the maximal ideal not lying in its square.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_IsRegularRing_uniqueFactorizationMonoid_of_isLocalRing.lean

import Mathlib

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
